      "point": "Britannica describes the blowpipe as a simple mouth-blown tube, while Wikipedia describes modern powered versions and blowtorches.",
      "quote_britannica": "To order to use the blow-pipe, the breath impelled through it is to be directed across the flame of a lamp or quantity, applying this orifice from which the dirt issues a little above on the upper end of the neck; and a jet of flame is thus formed as represented at fig. 8.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Starting in the late 18th Century, blowpipes have been powered by mechanisms, initially bladders and bellows, but now blowers, compressors and compressed gas cylinders are commonplace. While blowing air is effective, blowing oxygen produces higher temperatures, and it is also practical to invert the roles of the gasses and blow fuel through air. Contemporary blowtorches and oxy-fuel welding and cutting torches can be considered to be modern developments of the blowpipe."
